Linux"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each path is a ","type":"text"},{"code":"Path","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with the edges it takes, paired with its length. Paths are told apart by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"their edges, so parallel edges give distinct paths. Paths of equal length come in an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"unspecified order. The out-edges and their weights are copied when the sequence is made; each","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"path after the first then costs up to one shortest-path search per vertex on the path before","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"it. Taking a prefix, ","type":"text"},{"code":"prefix(k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", gives the k shortest simple paths.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each iterator starts over from the first path. Holds a copy of the graph, as","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"ShortestPathTree","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" does.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"kind":"symbol","variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/shortestpaths\/shortestsimplepaths"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"topicSections":[{"title":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/ShortestPaths\/documentation\/ShortestPaths\/ShortestSimplePaths\/Iterator"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Structures"},{"title":"Instance Methods","identifiers":["doc:\/\/ShortestPaths\/documentation\/ShortestPaths\/ShortestSimplePaths\/makeIterator()"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Instance-Methods"},{"title":"Default Implementations","identifiers":["doc:\/\/ShortestPaths\/documentation\/ShortestPaths\/ShortestSimplePaths\/Sequence-Implementations"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Default-Implementations"}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The simple paths 